dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: readv(2) with more than one iovec

Applies on top of dante-threads.patch (any order with the other patches
works; it only touches Rreadv() in lib/Rcompat.c).

Rreadv() copied the caller's first iovec into a one-element array but set
msg_iovlen to the caller's iovcnt. For a socksified socket Rrecvmsg() then
receives into msg_iov[0..iovcnt-1], i.e. it took whatever followed the
array on Rreadv()'s stack as iovecs and wrote received data to those
addresses. With libevent (evbuffer_read() reads with up to 4 iovecs) that
overwrites the caller's frame: musl's stack protector kills the program
(__stack_chk_fail, "general protection fault ... in ld-musl") as soon as
data arrives on proxied connections. Rreadv() now passes the caller's
iovecs.

Test: test/readvcheck.c (via test/run.sh).

--- a/lib/Rcompat.c
+++ b/lib/Rcompat.c
@@ -203,7 +203,6 @@
    int iovcnt;
 {
    const char *function = "Rreadv()";
-   struct iovec iov[1] = { { _iov->iov_base, _iov->iov_len } };
    struct msghdr msg;
 
    clientinit();
@@ -211,8 +210,14 @@
    slog(LOG_DEBUG, "%s: fd %d, iovcnt %d", function, d, iovcnt);
 
    bzero(&msg, sizeof(msg));
+   /*
+    * All of the caller's iovecs.  This used a one-element copy of the first
+    * iovec with msg_iovlen = iovcnt, so for iovcnt > 1 Rrecvmsg() took
+    * whatever followed on the stack as further iovecs and wrote received
+    * data there (e.g., into the caller's frame).
+    */
    /* LINTED operands have incompatible pointer types */
-   msg.msg_iov      = iov;
+   msg.msg_iov      = (struct iovec *)_iov;
    msg.msg_iovlen   = iovcnt;
 
    return Rrecvmsg(d, &msg, 0);
